<Layout title="From programmable crosspoints to circuit routing"><Header /><article><a href="/#notes">← Technical Notes</a><h1>From programmable crosspoints to circuit routing</h1><h2>Connections as a software problem</h2><p>Smartbreadboard explores selecting circuit connections in software to reduce repeated manual rewiring. The hardware design uses two MT8816 switching matrices, an I2C GPIO expander, a custom PCB and Arduino control firmware.</p><h2>Low-level switch control</h2><p>A crosspoint command selects a connection inside a switching matrix. The two devices provide 256 programmable crosspoints in total. This describes switch resources; it does not mean every requested circuit can be routed simultaneously.</p><h2>From commands to nets</h2><p>A higher-level interface could accept a request to connect circuit nodes and decide which switches to enable. Such a router must track occupied resources, shared paths and incompatible connections. It must also report when the requested circuit cannot be represented with the available hardware.</p><h2>Verification in stages</h2><p>Addressing and control-signal checks establish whether the control interface sends the intended commands. Electrical continuity, active-switch resistance, signal bandwidth and behaviour with several simultaneous connections require separate measurements.</p><h2>Current scope</h2><p>The portfolio documents PCB design, firmware and addressing/control-signal checks. Automatic routing and comprehensive electrical characterisation remain development work. The gallery records the breadboard development setup, routed PCB design, 3D render and assembled board.</p></article><Footer /></Layout>
